Chapter 17 – Part 4
Curtis took a sip of the coffee, long black braids shifting as he glanced toward the black smoke rising from the empty space above her collar.
“Yeah. He would say the same thing.” A short pause. His voice stayed careful. “But what about you? When you were texting me last night about Shinra. You sounded… I don’t know. Uncomfortable. In a way I wanted to invite you over, but I didn’t want to overstep my boundaries.”
The private channel went quiet for a moment. Celty’s posture did not change much, but the smoke above her collar thinned and then curled tighter, like a held reaction she could not fully disguise without a face.
When her real voice came back, it was lower than before.
“…You noticed.”
Not surprised. Just exposed.
“I told myself I was only reporting facts. Mei was there. They were close before. They were laughing.” A short pause. “But you’re right. It sat wrong. Not because Shinra is forbidden from having a past. Because I was in the other room listening to a version of ease I am not always sure I belong inside of… and the first person I reached for was you.”
The black smoke drifted sideways.
“That unsettled me more than the laughter did.” Another beat, more deliberate. “And for what it’s worth — if you had invited me last night, I don’t know if I would have come. Or if I would have come too quickly. So maybe not overstepping was safer for both of us then.”
Her gloved hands flexed once against her knees.
“But I’m here now. Which means the boundary moved whether either of us planned for it or not.”
Curtis looked at her over the coffee cup, mismatched eyes steady in the quiet flat.
“You can come here anytime you want when things get like this. You don’t have to be alone.”
The offer landed without ceremony — no performance, no pressure, just a door left deliberately open.
Black smoke rose more slowly from the empty space above Celty’s collar. For a moment she said nothing at all. The private channel remained open, but empty, as if she were testing the weight of the sentence before agreeing to carry it.
When her real voice finally came through, it was softer than most of the night had allowed.
“…That’s a dangerous thing to offer someone like me.”
A short pause.
“I spend a lot of time alone even when the apartment isn’t empty. It’s easier. Contained. If I start believing there’s a place I can come to when the walls get too honest… I might actually use it.” Another beat. The smoke drifted toward him slightly. “And I don’t know what that makes me in the middle of the life I already have.”
She sat with that longer than was comfortable, then inclined the empty line of her neck a fraction.
“But I heard you. And… thank you.” Quieter still. “If things get like that again, I may not pretend the only options are enduring it or putting the helmet on and riding until the feeling thins.”
A final pause.
“Don’t offer it unless you mean it, Curtis. Because I think I would come.”
Curtis set the coffee aside and reached out without making a production of it.
His hand found hers — warm, scarred at the edges, careful. Celty’s gloved fingers curled into his almost at once, not tentative so much as decided, the contact landing firmer than either of them named aloud.
“I’m going to the roof for a cigarette,” he said. “I can’t smoke in here.”
Celty’s real voice came through the private channel low and immediate.
“…Alright.”
She rose with him, helmet left behind on the table, black smoke trailing faintly upward from the empty space above her collar as she kept hold of his hand. No argument. No demand to finish the heavier conversation first. Just the quiet choice to follow.
Inside, under the sealed privacy of her own thoughts, the reaction was less composed.
The hand contact should have been simple. It wasn’t. It grounded her too fast — the same way his voice did when he answered her without a screen, the same way the flat felt less like someone else’s territory now that she was moving through it beside him. She hated how easily the loneliness of the previous night rearranged itself around this: a roof, a cigarette, a man who had said she did not have to be alone and then proven it by not letting go first.
This is how it starts, she thought, as they headed for the stairs.
Not with declarations. With doors left open and someone actually walking through them with you.
She tightened her fingers slightly around his and followed him up into the cooler night air.
On the stairs, with her gloved fingers still curled around his, Celty’s thoughts lost some of their edge and became harder to hold at a safe distance.
She had wanted to frame him as careful to the point of control — a man who rationed truth, managed timing, decided how much of himself other people got. That version was easier. It kept her anger organized. It made the sealed helmet, the delayed admission, and the half-answers look like strategy.
But strategy did not look like what she had seen in his flat.
Strategy did not stand bare to the waist and offer old wounds as proof of honesty.
Strategy did not go pale in the middle of a kitchen because a memory opened without permission.
Strategy did not say I trust you, please don’t make me regret it like the sentence itself could cut him if handled wrong.
The longer she stayed beside him, the clearer the softer reading became.
He was not closed because he was above attachment.
He was closed because attachment still had somewhere living to land.
Under the bluntness, under the capacity, under the city-facing calm, there was a man who had lost the one person who used to pull him back. A man who could survive torture and still get undone by the memory of what had been done to other people. A man who offered his roof, his hand, his number, his worst scars — and then braced, quietly, for the moment all of it might be used against him.
Vulnerable was not the first word most people would put on Curtis Nightstar.
It was the one that fit.
And that, more than the contradictions, was what made following him up the stairs feel dangerous. Not because he was playing her.
Because he was giving her places to touch him that could actually bruise.
The roof was cooler and wider than the flat below, city light washing the low walls in broken color. Curtis released her hand only long enough to fish out a cigarette, light it, and take the first drag. Smoke drifted from his mouth into the night as long black braids shifted against the black compression shirt.
He leaned against the ledge and looked over at her — helmet off, black shadow rising from the empty place above her collar, still somehow more present than most people with faces.
“So. Anything else you would like to know.” A short pause. “I’ll share what I can for now.”
Celty remained a step closer than strictly necessary, the private channel quiet while she watched him through the absence where her eyes would have been. When her real voice came, it was calmer than earlier, but no less direct.
“Then don’t give me the whole map. Just the part that explains the man in front of me.”
A thin ribbon of black smoke drifted sideways in the roof wind.
“Justice. What he was to you beyond brother and conscience. Whether leaving Appalachia ever actually left you, or if you just learned how to carry it into new cities. And why, after everything that taught you not to trust soft rooms, you keep building them for other people anyway.”
A shorter beat.
“You don’t have to finish any of it tonight. But those are the pieces I keep reaching for when I look at you.”
She angled the empty line of her neck toward him.
“Share what you can. I’ll stay for the incomplete version.”
Curtis took a longer drag from the cigarette, eyes on the city instead of her, long black braids shifting in the roof wind.
“Because Justice killed himself and betrayed me.” The words came out flat, but the flatness was only a surface. Underneath them something older and less defended scraped through. “He promised he would never leave my side….”
The rest of the sentence did not arrive. He left the broken promise standing in the night air on its own, smoke trailing from the cigarette as if the body needed something ordinary to do while the truth finished landing.
Celty went very still.
Black shadow-smoke above her collar tightened, then rose harder into the wind. For a moment the private channel held nothing at all — no analysis, no careful framing — only the sheer impact of what he had said. When her real voice finally came through, it was low and stripped of every earlier argument.
“…Curtis.”
Not a question. A recognition of the size of the wound.
“That’s not a small betrayal to put on a dead man. Especially not one who taught you how to care in the first place.” A short pause. The smoke drifted toward him, almost involuntarily. “He promised to stay. Then he made himself the one kind of absence you couldn’t chase, fight, or drag back by force.”
Another heavier beat.
“No wonder the soft rooms scare you. No wonder part of you still reaches for people anyway.” Her gloved hands flexed once at her sides. “You learned love as something that stays — and then learned it as something that can leave mid-sentence and call that the end.”
She did not step closer yet. She also did not step away.
“I’m sorry. Not in the empty way. In the way that means I understand why that promise still sits in you like an open wire.”
Curtis watched the cigarette burn down between his fingers, long black braids shifting against the black compression shirt as the next piece left him with less resistance than the last.
“I’m going to stop aging at 25. I’m not immortal. I can still die.” A short pause. The city lights kept moving under the roof ledge like none of this changed their work. “Justice was supposed to live alongside me as we faced the years to come. But now I’m left to wonder if this is now a curse… knowing everyone around me is eventually going to grow old and die.”
There it was.
A missing piece that suddenly made several others sit differently: the guarded softness, the way he could hold people carefully and still keep one foot turned toward the door, the strange patience and the strange panic living in the same body. Not just trauma. Not just loyalty to a dead brother. Time itself had been rewritten around him, and the one person meant to cross that rewritten span with him was gone.
Celty did not answer at once.
Black smoke rose harder from the empty space above her collar, caught and pulled sideways by the roof wind. In the private channel her silence had weight, the kind that meant something had finally locked into place.
When her real voice came, it was quieter than before and far more certain.
“…That changes the shape of you.”
Not accusation. Recognition.
“I kept reading your distance as only damage. Or caution. Or the aftereffect of being left once.” A short pause. “But this is different. You weren’t only abandoned by a person. You were abandoned into a future he was supposed to help you carry.”
The smoke drifted closer to him in the dark.
“I know what it is to remain while the world moves. Not the same way — I’m not claiming that — but enough to understand why the years ahead would start looking less like a gift and more like a sentence. Why every soft connection becomes both precious and dangerous. Why you keep asking yourself whether caring is compassion… or just arranging future grief in advance.”
She stood with him in that knowledge without trying to solve it.
“That isn’t a small missing piece, Curtis. That’s the frame under half of what I’ve been looking at.” A final, lower beat. “No wonder you treat time like it has teeth.”
Curtis took another drag from the cigarette and let the smoke leave him slowly, long black braids shifting in the roof wind.
“It’s why I’m scared to get too close. I’m 19 now, but when I’m 25 and forward…” He sighed, the sound thin around the edges. “I don’t know, Celty. It’s better to keep people at arm’s length at times. But I don’t know — when all’s said and done, what do I do? When they grow old and die. Do I constantly build new bonds and torture myself leading into years of insanity?”
The question hung over the city lights with no clean place to land.
Celty remained still beside him, black smoke rising from the empty space above her collar and tearing softly into the night air. When her real voice came through the private channel, it carried none of the earlier sharpness — only a grave, careful honesty.
“I don’t think there’s an answer that doesn’t cost you something.”
A short pause.
“If you keep everyone at arm’s length, you protect yourself from the specific grief of watching them age out of your life. But you also guarantee a different kind of damage: a life made of unfinished almosts. Rooms you never fully enter. People who needed you and only ever got the protected version.” The smoke drifted sideways. “If you keep building bonds anyway, yes — you will lose them. Again and again. That isn’t dramatic. That’s the arithmetic of what you’ve become.”
Another longer beat.
“What I don’t accept is that those are only two roads, and both end in insanity.” Her gloved hands rested against the ledge. “Some people stay with you in ways that are not only years spent side by side. Some bonds do not become meaningless just because one body stops. You already know that. Justice is still deciding half your conscience from the grave.”
She turned the empty line of her neck toward him more fully.
“I’m not going to tell you closeness is safe. It isn’t. Not for you. Maybe not for anyone who remains too long. But arm’s length is not the same thing as survival, Curtis. Sometimes it’s just a slower way of disappearing while you’re still alive.”
The black smoke thinned in the wind between them.
“If you ask me what you do… I think you choose carefully, not never. And you stop confusing the certainty of loss with proof that love was a mistake.”
Curtis finished the cigarette, crushed it out against the ledge, and let the last of the smoke leave his lungs in a thin stream. Long black braids shifted against the black compression shirt as he gave a small, weary nod.
“…Maybe you’re right.” A short pause. “So. Got your answers tonight? ’Cause I’m tired now. Talking about this makes me tired.”
Celty’s black smoke drifted lower in the roof wind, less agitated than before. When her real voice came through the private channel, it was quieter, almost gentle around the edges.
“Some of them. Not all. But enough that I understand the shape of you better than I did when I walked into your building.” A beat. “And fair enough. This isn’t light conversation. Anyone still standing upright after laying that much out has earned the right to be done for the night.”
She inclined the empty line of her neck toward the stairwell.
“Come on. Let’s go back down.”
They left the roof together.
The stairwell took their footsteps in a slower rhythm than when they had come up — no urgency, no argument left to outrun. Curtis kept close enough that their shoulders nearly shared the same line on the turns. Celty matched his pace without comment, black shadow-smoke rising faintly above her collar and thinning in the warmer indoor air as they descended. Neither reached for another heavy question. The quiet between them had changed shape: less confrontation, more the aftermath of two people who had finally stopped circling the truth long enough to touch it.
By the time they re-entered the flat, the city noise had fallen away behind sealed windows, and the yellow helmet still waited on the table where she had left it — a reminder that the night could end, if they let it, without putting every wall back exactly where it had been.
Elsewhere, the apartment was warmer and far less honest.
Shinra and Mei collected their clothes in the low light of his room, the earlier heat of the encounter already cooling into the practical after: belts, shirts, the quiet sounds of two people who had done this enough times to move around each other without ceremony. Mei laughed under her breath as she pulled her top back into place, pink hair falling loose over one shoulder.
“You’re serious. You two don’t really sleep in the same bed or do it?”
Shinra paused with his glasses halfway back onto his face, then smiled in that bright, sideways way he used when a subject was both scientifically interesting and personally inconvenient.
“Ah… define ‘really.’” He adjusted the frames and reached for his shirt. “Celty and I have a profound connection. Years of shared history, mutual devotion, an intimacy most people never achieve in a lifetime. Physicality is only one language among many, and ours has always been… more complicated than the standard arrangement.”
Mei gave him a look that said she was not buying the poetry.
Shinra’s smile thinned, then turned more candid around the edges.
“We don’t share a bed the way you’re implying, no. And the rest is rare. Carefully approached. Sometimes more theoretical than practiced.” He buttoned his shirt with neat, absent-minded precision. “She is not human. I am. That creates a certain… lag between what I feel and what the relationship can easily hold in a body. It doesn’t make what we have less real. It only makes it less simple.”
Mei arched a brow, still faintly amused, still sharp.
“So I get the simple version.”
Shinra’s expression flickered — fond, guilty, and not quite repentant.
“…You get a version that doesn’t require translation.” He met her eyes briefly. “Which is not the same thing as saying it’s the more important one.”
Outside the room, the apartment remained empty of Celty’s presence.
She was still across the city, in another man’s flat, with her helmet off and the night already rearranged around someone else’s honesty.
Back in the flat, Curtis dropped onto the couch and tilted his head back against the cushion, long black braids spilling over the black compression shirt as the last of the roof wind’s tension drained out of his shoulders. The coffee had gone cold. The city noise stayed politely outside the windows. For a moment he just breathed, eyes half-lidded, the heavy talk still sitting in his chest like weather that had not fully passed.
Celty remained standing near the table for a second longer, yellow helmet still set aside, black smoke rising quietly from the empty space above her collar. Then she moved closer and sat as well, not crowded against him, but near enough that the silence had company.
Her real voice came through the private channel, low and careful.
“…When you say you’re tired after talking about this — is it the memories that exhaust you, or the fact that you finally stopped carrying them alone for a few minutes?”
The question hung in the soft dark of the flat, not demanding, not gentle enough to be empty. Just precise enough to matter.
Curtis kept his eyes on the ceiling, long black braids resting against the cushion, voice quieter than it had been on the roof.
“Both. I’m an insomniac and have lived off limited sleep all of my life without problem. But talking about myself like that is exhausting. And I don’t realize how much I keep in until I talk about it.” A short pause. “Most of the time I’m talking to these walls.”
The flat seemed to acknowledge the line — empty spaces, clean surfaces, the kind of quiet that could keep a man company without ever answering back.
Celty sat with it for a moment, black smoke rising slow from the empty space above her collar. When her real voice came through the private channel, it was stripped of every earlier demand and left with something closer to recognition.
“…That explains the way you speak when you finally do open your mouth. Like the words have been waiting in an empty room with no one to stop them from getting heavier.”
A short pause.
“Walls don’t interrupt. They don’t flinch. They don’t ask what the story makes them responsible for. People do.” The smoke drifted slightly toward him. “So of course it exhausts you. You’re not just remembering when you talk. You’re putting weight into a living place and waiting to see whether it holds or collapses.”
Another quieter beat.
“For what it’s worth… these walls aren’t the only thing listening tonight. And I’m not asking you to empty the whole apartment in one sitting.” Her gloved hands rested loosely against her knees. “But if the reason you’re tired is that something actually left your chest for once, then that kind of tired might be earned.”
She inclined the empty line of her neck toward him in the soft dark.
“You can stop here. You already gave more than most people ever get from you.”
Curtis closed his eyes, long black braids resting against the couch cushion, the last of the night’s tension loosening from his shoulders.
“Yeah. I’ll just rest for a moment.”
His breathing evened out faster than most people’s would have after a conversation like that — not true sleep yet, but the deep, practiced quiet of someone who had learned how to put the body down even when the mind stayed half-awake. The flat settled around him. Coffee cold on the table. City noise distant beyond the windows.
Celty remained where she was.
Black smoke rose slowly from the empty space above her collar as the debate began, private and unwelcome in its honesty.
Leave.
That was the clean option. Put the helmet back on, take Shooter down into the street, return to the apartment before the night got any more complicated. Shinra would not question a long ride. He never did. She had built years of cover out of the city’s open roads.
Stay.
That option sat heavier. Curtis was still on the couch, eyes closed, the most unguarded she had ever seen him. Not asking her to remain. Not performing need. Just tired in the specific way of someone who had finally put too much truth into a room and could not immediately pick it all back up.
Her thoughts drifted, almost against her will, to older nights — Shinra’s college years, the times he had stayed over at Mei’s dorm and come home late with easy explanations. She had accepted those absences as ordinary then. Study. Friends. The social life of a human man. She had not examined them too closely. She still didn’t know how deep that old current truly ran.
If those nights had been allowed to exist without collapsing the life she shared with him, then why was this different?
She could stay.
She could tell Shinra she had been driving until morning.
He would buy it. He always bought the road.
Black smoke curled tighter above her collar.
The real question was not whether the cover would hold.
It was whether she wanted the cover — or whether she wanted the quiet of this flat, this couch, this man who had trusted her with the parts of himself he usually only told to walls.
Celty remained seated in the quiet, black smoke rising softly from the empty space above her collar as she watched the even line of Curtis’s breathing. For a moment it looked like she might let him drift without interruption.
Then her real voice came through the private channel, low enough not to startle, but clear enough that it asked to be answered.
“…Curtis.”
A short pause.
“If I stay a little longer — not for more questions, not for anything heavy — would that make this harder for you… or easier?”
The black smoke drifted sideways in the still air of the flat.
She had not put the helmet back on. She had not moved toward the door. The question sat between them exactly as it was: not a demand, not a confession, only the one practical truth underneath her private debate, waiting for him while his eyes were still closed.
Curtis didn’t open his eyes fully. He only lifted one hand from the couch cushion and motioned vaguely down the short hallway toward the spare bedroom, voice rough with fatigue.
“If you would like. Don’t force yourself to.”
Celty sat with the offer for a second, black smoke rising quietly from the empty space above her collar. Then she shook her head once.
Her real voice came through the private channel lower than before, and far more direct.
“Can I stay by you tonight?”
That made him open his eyes.
The surprise was plain and unguarded — a rare break in the usual control as mismatched eyes focused on her properly, long black braids shifting against the cushion when he lifted his head a fraction. For a moment he only looked at her, as if the question had landed in a part of him that had not been prepared for anyone to ask it without conditions, jokes, or retreat already built in.
The flat held the silence carefully.
Curtis blinked once, still caught on the shape of what she had actually requested:
Not the spare room.
Not distance with a polite door between them.
By him.
Curtis stared at her, long black braids resting against the cushion, mismatched eyes still caught halfway between fatigue and genuine surprise.
“…Huh?”
Celty did not retreat from it. Black smoke rose steadily from the empty space above her collar as she remained seated near him, the private channel quiet for a breath before her real voice returned, steadier than the request should have sounded.
“You heard me.”
A short pause.
“Not the spare room. Not across the apartment with a polite amount of distance so neither of us has to admit this night changed anything.” The smoke drifted slightly toward him. “By you. If you’ll allow it.”
Another beat, less armored than before.
“You said I didn’t have to be alone when things get like this. This is me taking that seriously. If the answer is no, say no. But don’t look at me like the question came out of nowhere. Not after everything you put in this room tonight.”
Curtis’s mouth tightened as he looked at her, long black braids shifting against the cushion.
“That’s not what I meant, and you know it.”
The line tried to reassert an older boundary. It only half-worked. He leaned his head back again, mismatched eyes on the ceiling while the practical meaning of her request finished landing in a body that had not been asked for this kind of closeness in a long time.
“…I—I haven’t slept next to a woman in a while.”
The admission came quieter than the refusal, and far more revealing.
Celty’s black smoke thinned, then curled once in the still air of the flat. When her real voice came through the private channel, it held no triumph and no mockery — only a careful, answering honesty.
“I know that’s not what you meant when you offered the first time.” A short pause. “I’m asking anyway.”
Another beat.
“And I’m not asking for anything beyond what I said. Not a performance. Not a conclusion. Not a claim on you by morning.” The empty line of her neck inclined slightly toward him. “Only not to put the helmet back on and pretend the only safe place left is the road… when you’re right here, and you already told me I didn’t have to be alone.”
The smoke drifted lower.
“If the fact that it’s been a while is the real hesitation, then say that. I can understand caution. I won’t understand you hiding behind a narrower version of the offer just because I took it more honestly than you expected.”
Curtis’s composure, usually so flat and controlled, finally frayed into something almost boyish around the edges.
“Yeah—yeah. Sure. Um.”
Celty reached out and took his hand before the awkwardness could harden back into retreat. Her gloved fingers closed around his with steady certainty.
“It’s fine, Curtis.”
The private channel carried the words softly, without pressure. Black smoke rose in a slow ribbon from the empty space above her collar as she held on, giving him a fixed point in the room that was not memory, not the roof conversation, not the long arithmetic of years and loss — just a present hand and a simple permission to stop performing competence for a moment.
Curtis looked down at their joined hands, long black braids falling forward over the black compression shirt, and exhaled like someone who had been bracing for a sharper outcome than the one he received. His fingers tightened once around hers in answer, rough and careful all at once.
“…Okay.”
Not polished. Not strategic. Just acceptance, small and human, as the flat settled around the two of them and the night finally stopped demanding explanations.
Curtis led her down the short hallway by the hand, long black braids shifting against the black compression shirt as he pushed the bedroom door open with his shoulder.
The room was larger than she might have expected. A queen-sized mattress sat low and clean against the far wall. A few packed boxes remained stacked near the corner, still sealed, the quiet evidence of a life only partially installed. Against the opposite side stood a computer desk with three dark screens. A small smart radio waited by the bed. The closet door was half-open just enough to suggest rows of unworn outfits hanging in deliberate order for later chapters of a life he had not fully stepped into yet. On a shelf near them, the sealed tech mask rested among the rest of his night gear like a second face put away for morning.
Curtis gave a dry little chuckle under his breath.
“Sorry. I still have stuff to unpack.”
Celty stood just inside the doorway, black smoke rising softly from the empty space above her collar as she took in the room — not messy, not empty, just incomplete in a way that matched him too well. When her real voice came through the private channel, it carried a faint edge of dry recognition.
“…That tracks.”
A short pause.
“You live like someone who paid for the whole future up front and then only moved into one room of it.” Her helmetless presence turned slightly toward the sealed boxes, then the triple screens, then the mask on the shelf. “It’s not untidy. It’s paused.”
Another quieter beat as her gloved fingers remained loosely in his.
“You don’t have to apologize for that. Half-unpacked still feels more honest than a place arranged to impress someone.” The smoke drifted toward the bed, then back to him. “This is fine, Curtis.”
Curtis’s gaze moved across the room — the sealed boxes, the triple screens, the clean queen mattress, the life only half installed — and something in his expression thinned.
“Most of this place came furnished. Justice chose it out before his death.” His voice stayed even, but the edges had gone quieter. “We thought it was a nice place to settle down as we got through college.”
The words left a hollow behind them. For a second his mismatched eyes started to pale the same way they had in the kitchen earlier, the present losing color as the old future — the one that still had two people in it — tried to overwrite the room. Long black braids hung still against his chest. His hand in hers loosened a fraction, not pulling away so much as drifting.
Celty felt the change immediately.
Her gloved fingers tightened around his, and her real voice cut into the private channel low and firm.
“Curtis.”
Not sharp. Present.
“Stay here.” Black smoke rose harder from the empty space above her collar as she stepped half a pace closer, enough for proximity to become an anchor. “Not in the version of this room that still has him unpacking boxes beside you. This one. Now. With the sealed cartons and the mask on the shelf and me standing in it.”
A shorter beat, steadier.
“He chose it. That can remain true without taking you out of the night you’re actually in.” Her grip held. “Come back.”
Under the pressure of her voice and the fixed point of her hand, the pale distance in his eyes stuttered, then slowly began to lose its claim on the room.
Curtis exhaled once, the last of the pale distance leaving his eyes.
“…Yeah.”
Then he simply let gravity take him, long black braids spilling across the sheets as he flopped face-down onto the queen mattress with all the grace of a man who had finally run out of posture. For a moment the room was only the sound of his breathing into the bedding and the quiet city beyond the window.
Celty remained standing beside the bed.
Black shadow-smoke gathered around her body in a controlled sweep, the riding suit dissolving and reforming with the ease of a familiar power. In its place she wore simple black nightwear — a matching bra and underwear, clean lines against pale skin, practical more than ornamental, the kind of change she could make in a breath when the day needed to end. She set the rest of herself down on the mattress beside him without announcement.
Curtis did not notice until the mattress shifted and the presence settled close.
Then he noticed.
He jerked so hard he lost the bed entirely, hitting the floor in an ungainly tumble of braids, black compression shirt, and pure system failure. From the carpet he stared up at her, face flushed dark under the room’s low light, mouth open on a complete absence of useful language.
“…Er.”
Celty propped herself up on one elbow and looked down at him over the empty space where her head should have been, black smoke rising in a slow, almost amused curl.
Her real voice came through the private channel, dry as kindling.
“…You asked me to stay by you.”
A short pause.
“If the reality of that was going to launch you off the mattress, you should have put it in the terms and conditions.” The smoke drifted toward the edge of the bed. “Are you coming back up, or is the floor part of your process?”
Curtis remained on the floor, long black braids spilled around his shoulders, face flushed so deep the color reached the tips of his ears.
“No — it’s not that!”
The denial came too fast, too pitched, and completely failed to recover any of his usual flat composure. For once there was no cartel calm, no street-ready bluntness, no carefully rationed vulnerability. Just a nineteen-year-old who had somehow survived forests, deportation, and worse, only to get ejected from his own bed by the simple fact of a woman lying down beside him in black nightwear.
Celty looked at him from the mattress and, privately, found the entire version of him almost unfairly adorable.
The thought arrived unwanted and clear: this was the same man who could throw a motorcycle through midair, stare down traffickers, and talk about years that would outlive everyone he touched — currently pink-faced on the carpet, scrambling for dignity like the floor might offer him a better argument. The contrast hit harder than the scars had. Strength she understood. This flustered, unarmored reaction was rarer. And, inconveniently, endearing.
Her real voice came through the private channel softer around the edges, though she kept a thin line of dry composure over it.
“…If it isn’t that, then your body is giving a very loud opposing statement.”
Black smoke curled once above her collar as she watched him.
“You’re allowed to be caught off guard, Curtis. You don’t have to pretend the man who just hit the floor is a completely different species from the one who invited me to stay.” A shorter beat, almost gentle. “Come back up. I won’t launch you again on purpose.”
Curtis, still on the floor, managed only a helpless,
“…Uh.”
Celty had apparently decided that was enough language from him for one night. She leaned over the edge of the mattress, caught him by the wrist and the front of the black compression shirt, and pulled with the easy strength of someone who had never needed a running start. Curtis left the carpet in an undignified half-tumble and landed under the covers beside her in a spill of long black braids, flushed skin, and residual panic. The blanket settled over both of them. Sudden warmth. Sudden proximity. No polite buffer left between the request and the reality of it.
He went rigid for half a second, then forced himself still, mismatched eyes wide in the low light of the bedroom.
Celty remained on her side facing him, black smoke rising softly from the empty space above her collar, one gloved hand still loosely curved near his arm as if to keep the escape route closed without making a cage of it.
And in the private quiet of her own thoughts, the comparison arrived whether she wanted it or not.
Shinra.
Shinra, who loved her in speeches and systems, who could turn devotion into analysis, who stayed and stayed and stayed — and who tonight was across the city in a simpler heat with Mei, a version of closeness that required no translation. Shinra, whose care had always contained a little ownership, a little fascination, a little of the boy who had grown up around her body as both person and subject.
Curtis was not that.
The distinction settled hard and clean beneath the covers: he had never once tried to manipulate her. Not into staying. Not into explaining herself. Not into becoming more convenient for his understanding of love. Even his worst habits — the distance, the delayed truths, the sealed helmet — had been about protecting himself, not arranging her. When he opened a door, he left the choice on her side of it. When he got overwhelmed, he said so. When she asked to stay by him, he flushed and fell off the bed instead of turning the moment into leverage.
No strategy.
No ownership dressed up as destiny.
Just a man who kept offering presence and then bracing for the cost of it.
Under the covers, with his body still tense beside her and the city muted beyond the window, that difference felt more intimate than the shared mattress.
Celty’s real voice came through the private channel very quietly.
“…Breathe, Curtis. I’m not going to vanish you into a moral crisis. We’re just lying down.”
A shorter beat, almost to herself as much as to him.
“That’s allowed.”
Curtis remained stiff under the covers, long black braids spilled across the pillow, mismatched eyes fixed somewhere near the ceiling as if direct eye contact might make the mattress more dangerous.
“…Uh. Celty. This is pretty intimate.”
Black smoke rose in a slow curl from the empty space above her collar. Her real voice came through the private channel without mockery, only a blunt curiosity softened by the hour.
“Have you ever been with a woman?”
The question should have been simple. It wasn’t.
Curtis’s expression changed at once — not into embarrassment this time, but into something tighter and older. A frown cut across his face as if a door he disliked had opened sideways in his memory. For a second the flush from earlier drained into a more complicated quiet.
“…Yeah. I had a girlfriend once.”
He left it there, but the way he said once carried the weight of an ending that had not been clean.
Celty watched the shift carefully, black smoke thinning as she tracked the difference between flustered proximity and actual ugliness rising under the surface. When she answered, her voice was lower, more measured.
“That face isn’t the face of someone remembering something gentle.”
A short pause.
“You don’t have to unpack her for me if the memory’s rotten. Especially not while you’re still recovering from everything else you already put on the table tonight.” Another beat, quieter. “I only asked because you’re looking at a shared bed like it might demand a history from you. It doesn’t. Being here isn’t a test of what came before.”
Her gloved hand remained near his arm under the covers, present without pressing.
“If she taught you the wrong lessons about closeness, they don’t get to set the terms of this room. Not unless you hand them that power.”
Curtis turned his head on the pillow, long black braids shifting against the sheets, and the tight line of old memory loosened enough for a soft smile to take its place.
“…You can’t just say things like that, Celty.”
There was no real protest in it. Only the quiet helplessness of a man who had spent too long expecting closeness to arrive with conditions, and was still learning what to do when it didn’t.
He shifted first — careful, almost testing — and then his arm settled around her. Celty answered the motion without hesitation, black smoke rising in a low ribbon from the empty space above her collar as she moved into the hold. Under the covers their bodies found a simpler arrangement than the conversation had: warmth, weight, the steady fact of not being alone in the room. His hand rested against the pale line of her back. Her gloved fingers curled lightly into the black compression shirt over his ribs. No demand under the contact. Just presence, finally held still long enough to mean something.
Celty’s real voice came through the private channel soft and close.
“…I can.”
A short pause.
“Especially when the alternative is watching you brace for a version of the night that isn’t happening.” The smoke drifted near his shoulder. “You keep waiting for the cost to arrive. For the lesson to turn ugly. For the room to become another place you have to survive.”
Her arm tightened a fraction around him.
“Not tonight. Tonight you can just be held. And so can I.”
Another quieter beat, almost reluctant in its honesty.
“…Don’t make me explain it more than that. I’m already further into this than I meant to be.”